<section id="overview">
<h2>Overview</h2>
<p>It starts with a feeling every driver dreads. The road is wet, the rain is steady, and as speed builds the steering goes strangely light. The wheel turns a little and the car keeps going straight. The tyres are no longer gripping the road at all — they are skimming on top of the water, like a flat stone skipped across a pond.</p>
<p>What is happening, in plain words, is this. A rolling tyre meets more water than it can push aside or squeeze through its tread grooves. The leftover water piles up in front of the tyre as a wedge. The faster you drive, the harder that wedge is rammed against the tyre, and the more it pushes back — upward. At some speed the upward push grows strong enough to carry the whole weight pressing that tyre onto the road. The tyre climbs onto the water, a thin layer separates rubber from pavement everywhere, and with that layer goes nearly all steering and braking force.</p>
<p>It matters because it arrives suddenly, at ordinary highway speeds, and it removes control rather than just reducing it. A tyre on water cannot be steered and can barely be braked. It also matters because the biggest lever is unglamorous and free to check: tyre pressure. A soft, underinflated tyre is lifted at a noticeably lower speed than a correctly inflated one.</p>
<div class="keynum"><div class="big">94 km/h</div><div><strong>The one number to remember.</strong><br>For a typical car tyre inflated to 220 kPa, experiments put the onset of this lifting at about 94 km/h — a normal motorway speed, in water only a few millimetres deep. The Theory section below shows where that number comes from, step by step, and the calculator lets you move it.</div></div>
<p>No formulas are needed to follow that story. The rest of this page builds it properly: first the wedge, then the pressure it creates, then the balance that decides the onset speed, then what the experiments actually measured.</p>
</section>

<section id="theory">
<h2>Theory</h2>
<p class="small">Symbols: <strong>p<sub>t</sub></strong> tyre inflation pressure, always <strong>gauge pressure</strong> on this page (the convention for tyre pressure) · <strong>p<sub>∞</sub></strong> ambient pressure of the undisturbed water · <strong>q</strong> dynamic pressure · <strong>Q<sub>w</sub></strong> water volume flow rate to clear · <strong>ρ</strong> water density, 998 kg/m³ at 20 °C · <strong>μ</strong> dynamic viscosity, 1.002 × 10⁻³ Pa·s at 20 °C · <strong>C<sub>Lh</sub></strong> hydrodynamic lift coefficient · <strong>h</strong> water-film depth · <strong>V</strong> vehicle speed · <strong>S</strong> contact-patch area · <strong>b</strong> tyre width, 0.205 m · <strong>L<sub>patch</sub></strong> contact-patch length, 0.15 m.</p>

<h3>(a) The water wedge and the contact patch</h3>
<p class="takeaway"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> water the tyre can't clear piles up as a wedge, and that wedge is what lifts it.</p>
<p>Work in the tyre's frame of reference: the tyre is stationary and the road, with its water film, streams toward it at speed V. The tyre touches the road over a small footprint — the contact patch, roughly 0.15 m long and as wide as the tyre (b = 0.205 m for a typical car tyre). Water that cannot escape sideways or through the tread grooves piles up ahead of that patch as a wedge. Inside the wedge the water slows down as it nears the tyre's leading edge. Slowing water down raises its pressure — and that pressure acts upward on the tread over the patch area S. As V grows, the wedge pressure grows fast, until it can support the whole wheel load and the tyre rides up onto the water.</p>

<h3>(b) Bernoulli and dynamic pressure</h3>
<p class="takeaway"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> slowing the wedge raises its pressure by q = ½ρV² — double the speed, four times the pressure.</p>
<p><strong>Assumptions, stated once:</strong> steady, inviscid, incompressible flow along a streamline, in the tyre frame. Bernoulli's equation between a point far upstream (pressure p<sub>∞</sub>, speed V) and the stagnation point at the wedge (speed ≈ 0) gives a pressure rise equal to the dynamic pressure:</p>
<div class="eq">p<sub>stag</sub> − p<sub>∞</sub> = q = ½ ρ V² &nbsp;&nbsp;with ρ = 998 kg/m³ at 20 °C</div>
<table><tr><th>Speed V</th><th>Dynamic pressure q</th></tr>
<tr><td>60 km/h</td><td>139 kPa</td></tr><tr><td>80 km/h</td><td>246 kPa</td></tr><tr><td>100 km/h</td><td>385 kPa</td></tr></table>
<p>Note that q at highway speed is the same order of magnitude as tyre inflation pressure — that coincidence is the whole problem. Double the speed and q quadruples, because it grows with V².</p>

<h3>(c) Lift and the square-root scaling</h3>
<p class="takeaway"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> hydroplaning starts when lift pressure equals tyre pressure, so onset speed grows with √(p<sub>t</sub>) — more pressure, later onset.</p>
<p>The hydrodynamic lift on the tyre is the wedge pressure acting over the patch, reduced by a lift coefficient:</p>
<div class="eq">L = q · S · C<sub>Lh</sub> &nbsp;&nbsp;·&nbsp;&nbsp; lift pressure = C<sub>Lh</sub> · q</div>
<p>For a rolling tyre with C<sub>Lh</sub> = 0.7, the lift pressure at 100 km/h is 0.7 × 385 = <strong>269.5 kPa (about 270 kPa)</strong>. The tyre pushes down on the road with roughly its inflation pressure acting over the same area, p<sub>t</sub>·S. Hydroplaning begins when lift balances that load, i.e. when q·C<sub>Lh</sub> = p<sub>t</sub>. Substituting q = ½ρV² and solving for V:</p>
<div class="eq">V = √( 2 p<sub>t</sub> / ( ρ C<sub>Lh</sub> ) )</div>
<p>At p<sub>t</sub> = 220 kPa this lift-balance speed is <strong>90.3 km/h</strong> rolling (C<sub>Lh</sub> = 0.7) and <strong>77.6 km/h</strong> sliding (C<sub>Lh</sub> = 0.95).</p>
<div class="note"><strong>Read this honestly:</strong> the √(p<sub>t</sub>) <em>scaling</em> is derived from Bernoulli. C<sub>Lh</sub> is <strong>not</strong> derived — it is an empirical fitting constant. Working backwards from NASA's measured coefficients implies C<sub>Lh</sub> ≈ 0.64 (rolling) and ≈ 0.88 (non-rotating). Literature values reported by van Es (2001), citing NASA Langley work, are ≈ 0.7 rolling and ≈ 0.95 sliding. The two sets <strong>do not agree exactly</strong>. The calculator uses the literature pair (0.7 / 0.95) for the lift-balance check and shows NASA separately, so the two can be compared rather than conflated.</div>

<h3>(d) The NASA empirical formula</h3>
<p class="takeaway"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> NASA's experiments give the same square-root rule with measured numbers — 94.3 km/h at 220 kPa for a rolling tyre.</p>
<p>Horne's NASA Technical Note D-2056 (1963) measured onset in spin-down and tow tests and fitted, with p<sub>t</sub> in kPa as gauge pressure:</p>
<div class="eq">V<sub>p,rolling</sub> = 6.36 √p<sub>t</sub> &nbsp;km/h &nbsp;&nbsp;·&nbsp;&nbsp; V<sub>p,non-rotating</sub> = 5.43 √p<sub>t</sub> &nbsp;km/h</div>
<table><tr><th>Tyre pressure p<sub>t</sub></th><th>Rolling onset</th><th>Non-rotating onset</th></tr>
<tr><td>180 kPa</td><td>85.3 km/h</td><td>72.9 km/h</td></tr>
<tr><td>220 kPa</td><td>94.3 km/h</td><td>80.5 km/h</td></tr>
<tr><td>240 kPa</td><td>98.5 km/h</td><td>84.1 km/h</td></tr>
<tr><td>280 kPa</td><td>106.4 km/h</td><td>—</td></tr></table>
<p>In the original imperial forms: 9 √p<sub>t</sub> knots and 10.35 √p<sub>t</sub> mph, with p<sub>t</sub> in psi. The 6.36 coefficient is the <strong>published SI form</strong>. The exact conversion of 9 knots is 6.35 — a difference of about <strong>0.17 km/h at 220 kPa</strong>. This page keeps the published 6.36 and says so, rather than silently changing it to 6.35.</p>

<h3>(e) Rolling vs non-rotating (a locked wheel)</h3>
<p class="takeaway"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> a locked, non-rotating wheel lifts earlier — 80.5 instead of 94.3 km/h at 220 kPa — and stays lifted.</p>
<p>A <strong>non-rotating</strong> wheel — locked under braking, or a landing wheel not yet spun up — hydroplanes at a <em>lower</em> speed than a rolling one: 80.5 km/h instead of 94.3 km/h at 220 kPa. Worse, once a locked wheel is hydroplaning it keeps hydroplaning until speed falls by other means, because nothing spins it back up into contact.</p>
<p>Attribution matters here: the non-rotating coefficient corresponds to 7.7 √p<sub>t</sub> knots, a figure from the <strong>Transport Canada AIP, as quoted in Transportation Safety Board of Canada report A02C0072</strong> — it is <em>not</em> from NASA TN D-2056 alone, and this page cites it that way.</p>

<h3>(f) Reynolds number and the squeeze-film problem</h3>
<p class="takeaway"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> over the patch, inertia rules so Bernoulli works — but a thin film also has to be squeezed out in ~6 ms, and viscosity can stop that.</p>
<div class="eq">Re = ρ V L / μ &nbsp;&nbsp;·&nbsp;&nbsp; μ = 1.002 × 10⁻³ Pa·s at 20 °C · 1.307 × 10⁻³ Pa·s at 10 °C</div>
<ul>
<li><strong>At 100 km/h:</strong> Re<sub>h</sub> = <strong>8.3 × 10⁴</strong> for a film of h = 3 mm, and Re<sub>patch</sub> = <strong>4.15 × 10⁶</strong> for L = L<sub>patch</sub> = 0.15 m. Over the patch, inertia dominates and viscosity is negligible — which is why the inviscid Bernoulli step in (b) works for the wedge.</li>
<li><strong>At 90 km/h (25 m/s):</strong> Re<sub>h</sub> = <strong>7.5 × 10⁴</strong> and Re<sub>patch</sub> = <strong>3.7 × 10⁶</strong>.</li>
<li><strong>Temperature note:</strong> at 10 °C, use ρ = 999.7 kg/m³ — a difference of only 0.17% from 998 kg/m³. Temperature reaches these results only through μ in the Reynolds numbers, which is why it is not a calculator input.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Squeeze film:</strong> in the thin-film regime the mechanism is not wedge pressure but time. A patch of tyre passes any point on the road in a contact time of about L<sub>patch</sub>/V — roughly 6 ms at 25 m/s. The water film must be squeezed out sideways within that time. Viscosity resists that squeezing, so on smooth surfaces a thin film can survive the pass and separate tyre from road even when the wedge calculation looks comfortable.</p>

<h3>(g) Tread and getting water out</h3>
<p class="takeaway"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> each tyre must clear up to ~17 litres of water per second; tread grooves are the escape route, and once water is deeper than the tread they can't help.</p>
<p>A tyre of width b meeting a film of depth h at speed V encounters water at a volume rate given by continuity:</p>
<div class="eq">Q<sub>w</sub> = V · b · h &nbsp;&nbsp;(b = 0.205 m — an upper bound only)</div>
<p>It is an <em>upper bound</em> because some water escapes sideways around the tyre rather than passing under it. Even so, the numbers are startling: <strong>17.1 L/s per tyre</strong> at 100 km/h with h = 3 mm; <strong>15.4 L/s per tyre</strong> at 90 km/h with h = 3 mm; and <strong>25.6 L/s per tyre</strong> at h = 5 mm and 25 m/s. Tread grooves are the escape channels for that flow. Tread depth enters this tool as a <strong>warning only</strong>: when h exceeds tread depth, the grooves are completely submerged and cannot add drainage capacity — the calculator warns in exactly that case, and tread feeds no onset equation.</p>

<h3>(h) Thin films and PAVDRN</h3>
<p class="takeaway"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> on thin films (under 2.4 mm) the PAVDRN model can predict a lower onset than NASA — the lower valid limit is the one that counts.</p>
<p>The Florida DOT PAVDRN model's thin-film branch predicts a hydroplaning speed from water-film thickness WFT alone:</p>
<div class="eq">HPS = 26.04 · WFT<sup>−0.259</sup> &nbsp;mph &nbsp;&nbsp;(WFT in inches)</div>
<table><tr><th>Film h</th><th>PAVDRN speed</th></tr>
<tr><td>0.5 mm</td><td>115.9 km/h</td></tr><tr><td>1.0 mm</td><td>96.9 km/h</td></tr><tr><td>2.0 mm</td><td>80.9 km/h</td></tr><tr><td>2.4 mm</td><td>77.2 km/h</td></tr></table>
<p>Rules followed strictly: this branch is valid <strong>only for h &lt; 2.4 mm</strong>. It is <strong>not used above 2.4 mm and is never extrapolated</strong>. Why not? PAVDRN's high-film branch would give 100.7 km/h at 2.4 mm — a discontinuity of about <strong>23 km/h</strong> at the same depth. That jump is the reason the high branch is not used here. Where the thin-film branch is valid, the <strong>controlling speed = min(selected NASA speed, PAVDRN)</strong>. NASA and PAVDRN are separate empirical fits with different tyre and pavement bases; neither is "the truth," and the lower valid estimate controls.</p>

<h3>(i) The three types compared</h3>
<p class="takeaway"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> same outcome — water between tyre and road — by three different mechanisms: wedge pressure, squeeze-film, or steam from a locked wheel.</p>
<p>Dynamic hydroplaning is the main story above. The other two types are variants of the same story — water surviving between tyre and road — with a different mechanism doing the work:</p>
<table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Mechanism</th><th>When</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Dynamic</strong></td><td>Inertial wedge pressure (the model above)</td><td>Deeper water, higher speed; onset scales as √(p<sub>t</sub>)</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Viscous</strong></td><td>Squeeze-film survival on smooth pavement / smooth tyres</td><td>Thin films; <strong>can occur at lower speeds than dynamic</strong> hydroplaning</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Reverted rubber</strong></td><td>A locked wheel's friction boils the film; steam lifts the tyre and the rubber reverts (softens/melts)</td><td>Locked-wheel braking on wet pavement — mainly an aviation concern</td></tr></table>

<h3>(j) Limits — what the models cannot do</h3>
<p class="takeaway"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> every number here is an estimate from a specific model — useful for understanding and comparing, never a guarantee for a real tyre on a real road.</p>
<ul>
<li>NASA's formula is an <em>onset estimate</em> from 1960s test tyres, valid when water depth exceeds tread drainage capacity. Real onset varies with tread pattern, pavement texture, load, and tyre construction.</li>
<li>The lift-balance derivation assumes the wedge stagnates fully and that load ≈ p<sub>t</sub>·S. Both are approximations; C<sub>Lh</sub> absorbs their error, which is exactly why it is empirical.</li>
<li>The <strong>Gallaway model</strong> (FHWA-RD-79-31) was consulted and <strong>not used</strong> in the calculator: the published forms consulted disagree on the water-film term. It is mentioned here only, as a consulted source — it appears nowhere in the calculator itself.</li>
<li>PAVDRN's thin-film branch says nothing at or above 2.4 mm, and neither NASA formula uses film depth or tread depth at all. A result below onset for the selected model says nothing about thin-film risk on a smooth surface.</li>
<li>Nothing on this page is a driving recommendation. The statuses in the calculator describe position relative to an estimated onset, nothing more.</li>
</ul>
</section>

