<section id="examples">
<h2>Worked examples</h2>
<div class="card"><h3 style="margin-top:0">Example 1 — A typical car at 100 km/h</h3>
<p>p<sub>t</sub> = 220 kPa, h = 3 mm, V = 100 km/h. NASA rolling V<sub>p</sub> ≈ <strong>94 km/h</strong> — the car is already above it. Check with Bernoulli: q = 385 kPa; lift pressure = 0.7 × 385 ≈ <strong>270 kPa &gt; p<sub>t</sub> = 220 kPa</strong>. Both methods agree the tyre is lifted. PAVDRN does not apply (h = 3 mm ≥ 2.4 mm).</p></div>
<div class="card"><h3 style="margin-top:0">Example 2 — The underinflated tyre</h3>
<p>Same car, tyre neglected down to p<sub>t</sub> = 180 kPa. NASA rolling V<sub>p</sub> ≈ <strong>85 km/h</strong> — about <strong>9 km/h lost from 40 kPa of underinflation</strong>. Because onset scales as √(p<sub>t</sub>), an 18% pressure loss costs about 9% of onset speed. Checking tyre pressure is the cheapest hydroplaning countermeasure there is.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h3 style="margin-top:0">Example 3 — A thin film, where PAVDRN controls</h3>
<p>p<sub>t</sub> = 220 kPa, h = 2 mm. NASA rolling gives <strong>94.3 km/h</strong>. PAVDRN (valid, since h &lt; 2.4 mm) gives <strong>80.9 km/h</strong>. The controlling speed is min(94.3, 80.9) = <strong>80.9 km/h, from PAVDRN</strong>.</p>
<p><strong>What does <em>not</em> follow:</strong> no tread conclusion can be drawn from that comparison — PAVDRN has no tread input, so the 94.3 vs 80.9 gap is a difference between two empirical models, not a tread effect. Tread is discussed only via the h &gt; tread-depth warning in the calculator.</p></div>
</section>

