<section id="about" class="about">
<h2>About this site</h2>
<p>This section holds the documentation for how the site was made. The tool above stands on its own; everything below is provenance.</p>
<h3>How it was built — a two-model process</h3>
<p>Two AI models were used. <strong>Muse Spark 1.3</strong> (Muse) drafted the plan and built this website. <strong>Claude Sonnet 5.5</strong> (<span style="font-family:'JetBrains Mono',monospace">claude-sonnet-5-5</span>) independently critiqued the plan — recomputing the numbers rather than taking them on trust — and later built an independent calculator implementation from the formulas and inputs only, with no expected answers shown to it. The two calculator implementations were then compared on the same test cases.</p>
<p>Attribution: physics and sources are as cited in References. Text, code, and diagrams were produced with the two models named above, under the direction of the site author, who verified the key claims against the primary sources (NASA TN D-2056, the FDOT PAVDRN report, and TSB A02C0072) before accepting them.</p>
<h3>Accept / reject log for the critique</h3>
<table><tr><th>#</th><th>Critique point</th><th>Decision</th><th>Reason</th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>“Agreement within 4%” between lift-balance and NASA is not independent — both scale as √p<sub>t</sub>; C<sub>Lh</sub> was effectively fitted</td><td><strong>Accept</strong></td><td>Correct. The site states the scaling is derived and C<sub>Lh</sub> is empirical (≈0.64/0.88 implied by NASA; 0.7/0.95 in van Es)</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>Worked Example 3 drew a tread conclusion PAVDRN cannot support; NASA-vs-PAVDRN relationship unexplained; check the 2.4 mm branch</td><td><strong>Accept</strong></td><td>Verified in the FDOT report: cutoff and discontinuity are real (77.2 vs 100.7 km/h at 2.4 mm). Example rewritten; controlling speed = min of valid models</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>Tread depth and water temperature affect no output; status thresholds undefined; onset wording must stay estimate-relative</td><td><strong>Accept</strong></td><td>Tread is warning-only (h &gt; tread); temperature cut as an input; statuses defined at a 10 km/h band with onset-relative wording</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>“Rolling vs locked” labels need checking against TN D-2056</td><td><strong>Accept in part</strong></td><td>The 7.7-knot figure is real but is Transport Canada AIP via TSB A02C0072, not TN D-2056. Relabelled “non-rotating” and cited correctly</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>Symbol clashes: p for both pressures; q vs Q</td><td><strong>Accept</strong></td><td>p<sub>t</sub>, p<sub>∞</sub>, q, Q<sub>w</sub> used throughout; Q<sub>w</sub> labelled an upper bound</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>Wording overstatements about viscous hydroplaning; missing Bernoulli assumptions and squeeze-film mechanism</td><td><strong>Accept (edits)</strong></td><td>Softened to “can occur at lower speeds”; assumptions stated once; squeeze-film paragraph added</td></tr>
<tr><td>7</td><td>Calculator scope too large</td><td><strong>Accept</strong></td><td>Trimmed to one chart, a single SI/US toggle, PAVDRN as a conditional panel, Q<sub>w</sub> as a small extra</td></tr>
<tr><td>8</td><td>Comparison contamination risk (don't show the second model the expected answers); video caption delay risk</td><td><strong>Accept</strong></td><td>The independent calculator build was given formulas and inputs only, no validation values; practice upload planned unlisted a day early</td></tr>
<tr><td>9</td><td>Missing process evidence: verbatim prompt, model names, full critique, rubric defined before build, “where the AI was wrong”</td><td><strong>Accept</strong></td><td>This section exists because of this point</td></tr>
</table>
<h3>Comparison outcome</h3>
<p>The independent calculator was checked against the validation cases (NASA at 180/220/240 kPa; q at 60/80/100 km/h; PAVDRN at 1 and 2 mm) — <strong>no numeric errors were found</strong> in either implementation; the two agree numerically. One logic difference surfaced, in Claude's favour: Claude keyed the margin and status to the NASA speed for the <em>selected</em> tyre state, while the first build here always used the rolling speed. <strong>Version kept:</strong> this site, with Claude's selected-state margin and controlling-speed logic adopted into its calculator.</p>
<h3>Where the AI was wrong</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Muse (early plan)</strong> presented a 4% theory-vs-NASA agreement as independent validation when the √p<sub>t</sub> scaling made it nearly automatic — a framing error, caught by Claude.</li>
<li><strong>Muse (early plan)</strong> wrote a worked example concluding tread mattered from a model (PAVDRN) that contains no tread variable — an invalid inference, caught by Claude.</li>
<li><strong>Muse (early plan)</strong> attributed the 7.7-knot non-rotating coefficient to NASA; it is Transport Canada's figure. Caught by Claude's prompt to check, confirmed by searching TN D-2056.</li>
<li><strong>Muse (calculator, first build)</strong> computed the margin and status against the NASA <em>rolling</em> onset regardless of the selected tyre state. A non-rotating tyre at 90 km/h with p<sub>t</sub> = 220 kPa would have shown “Below estimated onset” even though it is above the 80.5 km/h non-rotating onset. The comparison caught this; the calculator now uses the selected state, with controlling speed = min(V<sub>p,selected</sub>, PAVDRN where valid).</li>
<li><strong>Claude</strong> proposed a hysteresis/recovery-speed reading of the two coefficients that the sources do not state as a separate formula; the accepted wording follows the TSB's plainer statement instead. Claude also could not confirm the 2.4 mm PAVDRN cutoff from memory — it had to be verified in the FDOT report, and was real.</li>
</ul>
